<BaseLayout title="Boot" description="How SIGRUN goes from GRUB to a running Rust kernel: the multiboot2 header, 1 GiB huge pages, and the long-mode transition.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Subsystem 1 · boot</p>
  <h1>Boot: from GRUB to a running Rust kernel</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The CPU starts in 32-bit mode with paging off. SIGRUN's own assembly builds the page tables, flips on
    long mode, and hands control to Rust — all in <code class="mono">kernel/src/arch/x86_64/boot.rs</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Why boot is hard</h2>
  <p>
    An x86_64 CPU doesn't power on in 64-bit mode. It starts in <strong>16-bit real mode</strong> (via the
    BIOS), the bootloader (GRUB2) puts it in <strong>32-bit protected mode</strong> with paging off, and only
    then can <em>we</em> take over. Entering long mode is a choreographed sequence: build page tables, enable
    PAE, load CR3, set <code>EFER.LME</code>, then set <code>CR0.PG</code> — and the moment paging turns on
    with LME active, the CPU is in 64-bit mode. SIGRUN does all of this in one <code class="mono">global_asm!</code>
    block.
  </p>

