  <h2>Step by step</h2>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>1 · The multiboot2 header</h3>
    <p>
      GRUB scans the first 32&nbsp;KB of the ISO image for a magic value <code class="mono">0xE85250D6</code>.
      SIGRUN emits it from a dedicated <code class="mono">.multiboot2_header</code> linker section placed first
      in the image, with a checksum that makes the sum of the first four words zero:
    </p>
<pre><code><span class="c">// boot.rs — global_asm!, Intel syntax</span>
".section .multiboot2_header, \"a\"",
".align 8",
"_mb2_start:",
".long 0xe85250d6",<span class="c">      // magic</span>
".long 0",<span class="c">                   // architecture: i386</span>
".long _mb2_end - _mb2_start",
".long -(0xe85250d6 + 0 + (_mb2_end - _mb2_start))",<span class="c">  // checksum</span>
".short 0", ".short 0", ".long 8",<span class="c">   // end tag</span></code></pre>
    <p>
      GRUB then loads the ELF, zeroes the BSS (which is where the boot stack and page tables live — declared
      <code class="mono">@nobits</code>), and calls <code class="mono">_start</code> with
      <code class="mono">EAX = 0x36d76289</code> (multiboot2 magic) and <code class="mono">EBX</code> = physical
      address of the multiboot2 info struct.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>2 · Check the CPU, build the tables</h3>
    <p>
      After <code class="mono">cli</code> and installing a 64&nbsp;KB boot stack, the code verifies long mode
      exists with <code class="mono">cpuid</code> leaf <code class="mono">0x80000001</code> (bit 29 of EDX =
      LM). Then it writes one PML4 entry and four PDPT entries — each PDPT entry is a
      <strong>1&nbsp;GiB huge page</strong> (bits <code class="mono">0x83</code> = present + writable + huge):
    </p>
<pre><code><span class="c">// identity-map first 4 GiB with 1-GiB huge pages</span>
"lea eax, [_boot_pdpt]",
"or  eax, 3",
"mov dword ptr [_boot_pml4],     eax",<span class="c">   // PML4[0] → PDPT (present|write)</span>
"mov dword ptr [_boot_pml4 + 4], 0",
"mov dword ptr [_boot_pdpt +  0], 0x00000083",<span class="c">  // 0–1 GiB</span>
"mov dword ptr [_boot_pdpt +  8], 0x40000083",<span class="c">  // 1–2 GiB</span>
"mov dword ptr [_boot_pdpt + 16], 0x80000083",<span class="c">  // 2–3 GiB</span>
"mov dword ptr [_boot_pdpt + 24], 0xC0000083",<span class="c">  // 3–4 GiB (LAPIC @ 0xFEE00000 lives here)</span></code></pre>
    <p>
      Why four PDPT entries and not a PD/PT tree? Because a PDPT entry with the
      <code class="mono">HUGE_PAGE</code> bit covers a full GiB — four of them cover the entire low 4&nbsp;GiB,
      which is everything the boot code and the kernel's identity-mapped region need.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>3 · The long-mode switch</h3>
<pre><code><span class="c">// Enable PAE, point CR3 at the PML4, set EFER.LME, then flip CR0.PG</span>
"mov eax, cr4",
"or  eax, 0x20",<span class="c">              // CR4.PAE</span>
"mov cr4, eax",
"lea eax, [_boot_pml4]",
"mov cr3, eax",<span class="c">               // page-table base</span>
"mov ecx, 0xC0000080",<span class="c">        // EFER MSR</span>
"rdmsr",
"or  eax, 0x100",<span class="c">             // EFER.LME bit 8</span>
"wrmsr",
"mov eax, cr0",
"or  eax, 0x80000001",<span class="c">        // CR0.PE | CR0.PG — paging ON ⇒ long mode</span>
"mov cr0, eax",
<span class="c">// far jump: selector 0x08 (our 64-bit code segment), target _boot_64_entry</span>
".byte 0xEA",
".long _boot_64_entry",
".short 0x08",</code></pre>
    <p>
      The far jump is the switch: with paging on and LME set, the CPU is executing in 64-bit mode the instant
      <code class="mono">CR0.PG</code> is set, but the instruction stream was encoded as 32-bit — so the far
      jump (encoded raw as <code class="mono">0xEA</code>, because the Intel-syntax mnemonic differs between
      assemblers) reloads CS with our flat 64-bit code segment and lands on 64-bit code.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>4 · Hand over to Rust</h3>
    <p>
      <code class="mono">_boot_64_entry</code> flushes segment-register caches and calls
      <code class="mono">kmain_from_multiboot2(magic, info_ptr)</code> using the System V calling convention
      (arguments in <code class="mono">RDI</code>/<code class="mono">RSI</code> — the values saved from
      EAX/EBX, now zero-extended). That function validates the magic, builds a
      <code class="mono">BootParams</code> struct (kernel physical/virtual start at
      <code class="mono">0x100000</code>, size computed from the linker symbol
      <code class="mono">__kernel_end</code>), and calls <code class="mono">kmain()</code>, which initializes
      each subsystem in order and finally enters the idle loop (see
      <a href="/scheduler">Scheduler</a>).
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="qa">
    <h2>Interview Q&amp;A: boot</h2>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">CORE</span>Why do you need assembly at all? Why can't the first Rust function do this?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          Because Rust — even <code class="mono">no_std</code> — compiles against an assumed environment: it
          needs a stack, and before we build one the CPU is running on whatever GRUB left us with paging off,
          in 32-bit mode. The entry point must set up its own stack, verify CPUID, build page tables by writing
          raw memory, and reprogram control registers — all things a high-level function can't express without
          either inline asm or the same assembly. So the 32-bit prologue is one
          <code class="mono">global_asm!</code> block, and the first thing Rust does
          (<code class="mono">serial::init()</code> for logging) happens after the transition.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">CORE</span>Why 1&nbsp;GiB huge pages instead of mapping with 4&nbsp;KiB pages?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          Boot-time simplicity. With 1&nbsp;GiB pages, the entire first 4&nbsp;GiB — kernel, multiboot info,
          the LAPIC's MMIO at <code class="mono">0xFEE00000</code>, everything — needs just 1 PML4 entry + 4
          PDPT entries, and the CPU's page-walker stops at PDPT. With 4&nbsp;KiB pages we'd need a million
          PTEs and four levels of tables before we could even run. It's also safe here: the physical addresses
          genuinely correspond to RAM/MMIO in identity mapping, and a real kernel refines this later (the
          memory subsystem allocates page tables from the frame allocator).
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="qa-followup">
        <b>FOLLOW-UP</b> "When would 1 GiB huge pages be wrong?" — Wasting address space or permissions
        granularity: you can't give ring-3 just a page inside a 1 GiB mapping, and you can't mark individual
        pages no-execute. SIGRUN solves this in the demo by flipping the U/S bit on whole PML4/PDPT entries —
        deliberately a hack.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">GOTCHA</span>Why does the linker need <code class="mono">-C relocation-model=static -C code-model=kernel</code>?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          Our 32-bit boot code writes absolute addresses into page-table entries
          (<code class="mono">lea eax, [_boot_pdpt]</code>), which lld encodes as
          <code class="mono">R_X86_64_32</code> relocations. With the default PIC/pie code model, lld rejects
          those. The flags tell rustc to generate non-relocatable code whose absolute addresses fit in 32 bits —
          exactly what 32-bit instructions need. The <code class="mono">.cargo/config.toml</code> applies them
          per-target for <code class="mono">x86_64-unknown-none</code>, and removing them breaks the build.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">GOTCHA</span>Why does <code class="mono">qemu -kernel</code> not work, and why must it be an ISO?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          QEMU 5+ refuses to load 64-bit ELF kernels directly via <code class="mono">-kernel</code>; the
          supported path here is a bootable ISO. SIGRUN's script builds <code class="mono">sigrun.iso</code>
          with <code class="mono">i686-elf-grub-mkrescue</code> (BIOS/legacy GRUB — the Homebrew
          <code class="mono">x86_64-elf-grub</code> package only ships an EFI target and silently fails to
          boot). This is also why the abandoned <code class="mono">boot/</code> UEFI loader exists: it was an
          alternative entry convention (<code class="mono">fn(&amp;BootParams)</code>) incompatible with the
          multiboot2 <code class="mono">_start</code> signature.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">DESIGN</span>What is the very first thing that can be tested after boot?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          Serial output. <code class="mono">log::early_init()</code> initializes COM1 (115200 baud, 8N1) before
          anything else, so every boot message is observable — the run script greps the serial stream for
          "SIGRUN kernel running" as the boot test, and the test exits 0 only if it appears.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

